"""One running analogy for CMO Midterm 3 (topics 09-19): you are the pilot of an airplane.
Legal airspace = feasible set, altitude = objective f (gravity = -grad f), mountain walls =
constraints g <= 0, wall push = multiplier mu, air-traffic control = projection operator."""
A = "<strong>The airplane analogy.</strong> "

_TXT = {
1001: r"Two rival airspaces (say a military zone and your civilian corridor) that do not overlap and are both convex. Air-traffic control can always draw one flat, infinite 'do-not-cross' wall that puts your corridor entirely on one side and the military zone entirely on the other.",
1002: r"The mountain wall you are parked against: a flat cliff face that touches your legal airspace at exactly your parking spot $x_0$, with the whole of the airspace lying on one side of it. That cliff face is the supporting hyperplane at the boundary point.",
1003: r"Gravity $b$ is pulling on your plane; the columns of $A$ are the normals of the mountain walls, and walls can only push (never pull). Either the wall pushes can add up exactly to cancel gravity ($Ax=b,\ x\ge0$) and the plane stays parked, or there is a heading $y$ that no wall blocks ($A^Ty\ge0$) along which gravity still drags you downhill ($b^Ty<0$). Exactly one of the two stories is true.",
1007: r"Farkas with numbers is a parking check. Write gravity $b'$ and the wall normals. If $b'$ lies inside the cone of wall pushes, the walls can hold the plane. If it sits outside, exhibit one heading $y$ that every wall allows but that gravity favours: that single $y$ is the certificate that the plane rolls away.",
1004: r"Either some heading takes you strictly away from every wall at once ($Ax<0$: open sky in all directions), or the wall pushes can balance each other out with non-negative weights ($A^Ty=0,\ y\ge0,\ y\ne0$): you are boxed into a canyon whose walls cancel exactly, with no escape heading.",
1005: r"The legal headings from your parking spot form a cone $K$. The polar cone $K^\circ$ is the set of 'gravity pulls' that make every legal heading go uphill or flat: pulls that can never make the plane roll anywhere legal. At a minimum, $-\nabla f$ must lie in $K^\circ$.",
1006: r"You are wedged in a mountain corner touching two walls. Gravity $-\nabla f$ pulls you into the corner, the two walls push back with forces $\mu_1\nabla g_1$ and $\mu_2\nabla g_2$, and Farkas guarantees the pushes can be chosen non-negative so that the net force is zero: that is the KKT equation.",
1101: r"The four KKT checks are the four things a parked plane must satisfy: gravity is cancelled by wall pushes ($\nabla f+\sum\mu_j\nabla g_j=0$), you are inside legal airspace ($g\le0$), walls only push, never pull ($\mu\ge0$), and a wall you are not touching pushes with zero force ($\mu_jg_j=0$).",
1102: r"Each mountain wall is an on/off switch. A wall across the valley ($g\lt0$, not touching) is OFF: $\mu=0$. A wall your wing is resting on ($g=0$) is ON and may push back with $\mu\gt0$. You never have both a gap and a push.",
1103: r"In a convex valley with flat walls there is only one lowest parking spot. If you are parked where the wall pushes cancel gravity (KKT holds), no deeper valley exists anywhere else in the legal airspace, so this stationary spot is already the global minimum.",
1104: r"LICQ says the walls you are touching push in independent directions. If two touching walls have normals pointing the same way (a cusp or a knife-edge), the pilot cannot tell how gravity splits between them: the push forces are not unique and may not exist at all, so KKT can fail at a true minimum.",
1105: r"Slater's condition: the legal airspace has real volume, so there is at least one point strictly inside every wall ($g\lt0$). It is a hangar with room to move, not a zero-thickness corridor squeezed between walls. With that breathing room, strong duality holds.",
1106: r"Case analysis is the pilot's two guesses. Case 1: assume you are floating free in the open (wall push $\mu=0$); if the lowest open-air point lies outside legal airspace, that guess is dead. Case 2: assume you are pressed against the wall; solving the force balance gives a positive push $\mu=8/5$, so the guess is consistent.",
1201: r"The runway is the tangent subspace $M$: the only headings you can roll along while keeping your wheels on the ground. The terrain may fall steeply away on both sides of the runway (negative curvature in the full space), but what matters is that the runway itself is level or curves upward, i.e. $d^\top\nabla^2\mathcal L\,d\ge0$ for $d\in M$.",
1202: r"Strict version: along every runway heading in the critical cone the ground curves strictly upward under your wheels, like the bottom of a bowl-shaped runway. Then your parking spot is a strict local minimum, even if the surrounding terrain drops off sideways.",
1203: r"The bordered Hessian is the instrument-panel shortcut. You do not parametrise the runway; you border the Hessian with the wall normals, and the sign pattern of its trailing minors tells you whether the runway dips at your parking spot (a minimum) or peaks (a maximum).",
1204: r"If every wall you touch pushes strictly ($\mu\gt0$), the plane is pinned and the only legal motions are the headings that slide along all those walls: a subspace. If a touching wall pushes with exactly zero, you can peel away from it in some headings and the critical cone is a proper cone, not a subspace. In 2D, two strictly pushing walls leave zero freedom.",
1205: r"A banked, curved runway. Even if the terrain looks flat or hilltop-like in the ambient space, the curvature of the wall supplies an extra centripetal push, so the right curvature to check is that of the Lagrangian, $\nabla^2 f+\sum\mu_j\nabla^2 g_j$, restricted to the runway.",
1206: r"The plane circles a circular no-fly ring and you want the lowest altitude $x_1+x_2$ on the ring. The lowest point is south-west at $(-1/\sqrt2,-1/\sqrt2)$: every step along the ring from there climbs, which is exactly what the bordered Hessian sign test confirms.",
1301: r"Wind blows you out of legal airspace. The closest legal point $P(p)$ is where you reach the boundary by flying the shortest path. Every other legal point $y$ makes an obtuse (or right) angle at $P(p)$ with the line back to you: $(p-P(p))^\top(y-P(p))\le0$.",
1302: r"Two off-course planes are each guided by ATC to their nearest legal spot. The two landing spots are never farther apart than the two planes were: projection never stretches distances, it can only squeeze them together.",
1303: r"ATC dropping you onto a flat ceiling plane (hyperplane) sends you straight along its normal. For a half-space of legal altitudes: if you are already on the allowed side you stay put, otherwise you are dropped along the normal onto the wall.",
1307: r"A box is a corridor with an altitude floor and ceiling and lane limits: ATC clamps each coordinate independently. A ball is a circular holding zone around the airport: if you are outside, steer straight at the centre until you touch the circle's edge, otherwise stay.",
1304: r"Any wind vector splits into a part pointing inside the legal cone ($K$) and a part pointing out of it (the polar cone $K^\circ$), and the two parts are perpendicular. One way of resolving wind into 'with the airspace' and 'against the wall' is the Moreau decomposition, like $x=x_++x_-$ in one dimension.",
1305: r"Two overlapping airspaces. Plain alternating ATC projections drift along the crease and end at some point in the overlap, not the nearest one. Dykstra's algorithm remembers the sideways correction from each bounce, so the plane lands on the closest point of the overlap.",
1306: r"A fixed fuel budget (total exactly 1) shared among tanks. Shave one common level $\tau$ off every tank reading, clipping at empty: the tanks that stay above the level keep their excess, the rest go dry, and $\tau$ is chosen so the leftover fuel sums to 1. That is the simplex projection.",
1401: r"Follow gravity downhill one step. If that carries you out of legal airspace into the mountain, ATC immediately projects you back to the nearest legal point: you slide along the wall instead of crashing through it.",
1402: r"At the optimum, gravity pushes the plane into the mountain wall with full force, but ATC projects you straight back to the same parking spot: $P(x^*-\alpha\nabla f(x^*))=x^*$. A point that does not move under one gradient-projection step is exactly a stationary parking spot.",
1403: r"The glide path to the lowest legal parking spot. On a merely convex (smooth) valley the approach is sublinear, $O(1/k)$. In a strongly convex bowl each leg shrinks the remaining distance by a fixed factor (linear rate). The mountain walls do not slow the glide rate.",
1404: r"Armijo along the arc: as you shorten the step $\alpha$, the points $P(x_k-\alpha\nabla f)$ trace an arc that bends along the mountain wall. You try points on that arc and accept the first one that gives a big enough altitude drop, the sufficient-decrease check.",
1405: r"On a curved boundary (a banked runway), flying straight ahead along the tangent takes you off the surface. Restoration is the fix: descend along the normal to put your wheels back on the curved surface before the next leg.",
1406: r"The ground is the altitude floor $x\ge0$. After each gravity step any negative coordinate is clamped to the ground: $x_{k+1}=\max(0,x_k-\alpha\nabla f(x_k))$. The plane never sinks below the tarmac.",
1501: r"Lock down the walls you are touching and treat them as rails: your motion must satisfy $A_{\mathcal W}p=0$, so you only solve for sliding along the runway between the walls. That is the equality-constrained subproblem.",
1502: r"Rolling along the runway toward the target, a mountain wall ahead stops you before you reach it: the step length is $\alpha\lt1$, you halt at the wall, and add that wall to the working set (the list of walls you are touching).",
1503: r"Parked against a wall, the force calculation says the wall would have to pull you (a negative multiplier). Walls cannot pull, so gravity actually wants you off that wall: release the brakes, drop that wall from the working set and take off away from it.",
1504: r"Every leg either hits a new wall or lands at the lowest point of the current runway, and every leg lowers altitude, so you never return to a working set you already used. There are only finitely many wall subsets, so the flight must finish in finitely many legs.",
1505: r"Degenerate corner: three or more walls meet at one point, so the plane sits on the tarmac with step $\alpha=0$ while the tower swaps which walls are officially in the working set. An anti-cycling rule (a fixed order for the tower to follow) stops it swapping in circles forever.",
1506: r"A beacon (the unconstrained minimiser) lies outside the legal airspace. The plane slides along one wall toward it until another wall stops it, drops walls whose pushes turn negative, and finally parks at the legal point nearest the beacon.",
1601: r"Move a mountain wall by $z$ and record the lowest legal altitude $\omega(z)$ you can reach. Slide a flat ruler (a hyperplane of slope $-\mu$) up from underneath this altitude curve until it touches it: where the ruler crosses the $z=0$ pole is the dual value $\phi(\mu)$.",
1602: r"Any wall price $\mu\ge0$ gives a floor beneath the lowest legal altitude. The best floor you can build (the dual optimum) can never rise above the true lowest legal altitude (the primal optimum): that gap is the duality gap.",
1603: r"With a convex valley and Slater's room to move, the best floor you can build touches the lowest legal altitude: zero gap. The pilot descending from above and the tower raising a floor from below meet at the same altitude.",
1604: r"For each fixed flight plan $x$, the quantity 'altitude + $\mu\cdot$wall violation' is a straight line in the price $\mu$. The dual function takes the lowest of all these lines at each $\mu$, and a pointwise minimum of straight lines is always concave, however wild the terrain.",
1605: r"The multiplier is a shadow price. If moving a mountain wall back by one metre of extra airspace lowers your lowest legal altitude by $\mu^*$, then $\mu^*$ is what the wall's push is worth per metre of room.",
1606: r"Two opposing traffic flows must be separated. Draw the widest possible no-fly strip between them: the strip's width (the margin) is set only by the aircraft that come closest to its edges, so maximising the margin is minimising $\tfrac12\|w\|^2$.",
1608: r"Only the planes touching the edges of the buffer strip (the support vectors) carry non-zero weight $\alpha_i$ in the dual; every other aircraft has $\alpha_i=0$ and could be removed without moving the strip. The kernel trick means the radar only needs distances and dot products between aircraft, not their coordinates.",
1701: r"Instead of checking every possible flight plan, the Wolfe dual restricts to equilibrium states: the positions where gravity is exactly balanced by wall pushes ($\nabla_x\mathcal L=0$). Among those equilibria you then maximise the Lagrangian over the wall pushes.",
1702: r"For a convex valley, the best floor built from equilibrium states touches the lowest legal altitude: Wolfe duality has zero gap, so the cockpit and the control tower report the same number.",
1703: r"Cockpit and tower look at the same flight. The cockpit minimises the fuel burn $\tfrac12x^\top Qx$ while the tower hedges with resources $b$ and pays the same quadratic cost with the opposite sign. At the optimum their totals meet.",
1704: r"Equilibrium ($\nabla_x\mathcal L=0$) means forces balance, but a force-balanced plane can sit at the top of a mountain peak or on a saddle. If the valley is not convex, trusting equilibrium builds a false floor, so the Wolfe dual is invalid.",
1705: r"The tower raises the wall price when the plane violates the wall ($g_j\gt0$, flying into the mountain) and lowers it when there is slack ($g_j\lt0$), never letting it go negative: $\mu_{k+1}=\max(0,\mu_k+\alpha g_j(x_k))$.",
1706: r"Spread a fixed fleet of aircraft across airports so that the total fuel budget is fixed. Maximising entropy under a fixed energy constraint gives the exponential (Boltzmann) distribution, and the wall price is the inverse temperature.",
1801: r"Airline vs airport authority. The airline minimises the cost $c^\top x$ of a flight schedule that meets demand $b$. The authority picks landing fees $y$ to maximise revenue $b^\top y$ while keeping every route no more expensive than the airline's cost ($A^\top y\le c$) so the airline still agrees to fly.",
1807: r"A plane can only be pinned in place at a corner of the polygonal airspace, where enough walls meet in independent directions that there is nowhere to slide. Those corners are the basic feasible solutions.",
1802: r"With a linear altitude (a tilted flat sheet over the polygonal airspace), the lowest point of the sheet over the polygon is always at a corner of the polygon, so the best parking spot is a vertex.",
1803: r"Complementary slackness is the airline's rulebook. If a route is flown ($x_j\gt0$), its fee exactly equals its cost. If the fee is below cost ($c_j\gt A_j^\top y$), nobody flies it ($x_j=0$). If a resource wall is not touched, its price $y_i$ is zero.",
1804: r"If no legal flight plan exists (the primal is infeasible), Farkas gives a heading $y$ that no wall blocks along which you can keep improving forever, so the dual can be pushed without bound: infeasible primal means unbounded dual.",
1805: r"At each airport corner the pilot looks at the neighbouring airports along the edges, hops to one that is lower, and stops when no neighbour is lower: that corner is the optimum.",
1808: r"Instead of scraping along the walls from corner to corner, fly down the centre of the corridor (the central path), a safe distance from every wall, and let the clearance shrink toward zero as you approach the destination corner.",
1806: r"The pilot in the cockpit and the tower with its price list reach the same total cost: the primal and dual optimum are equal, the strong duality of LP.",
1901: r"Restricted airspace with fines. Inside legal airspace ($g\le0$) you fly free; the moment you cross into the forbidden zone, the fine $c\,[\max(0,g)]^2$ grows quadratically. As the fine stiffens ($c\to\infty$) you are forced back to the boundary.",
1902: r"An invisible repulsive field around the mountains: the closer you get to a wall, the more the barrier $-\mu\ln(-g)$ blows up, so you can never touch the boundary and always fly strictly inside legal airspace.",
1903: r"With a huge fine $c$ the valley becomes a razor-thin canyon: extremely stiff across the wall and flat along the runway, so the pilot zig-zags between the canyon walls instead of making forward progress: the Hessian has condition number growing with $c$.",
1904: r"At each penalty iterate the plane sinks slightly into the forbidden zone. Fine times depth gives the wall's push force: $\lambda^*\approx c\,\max(0,g)$ or $c\,h(x_k)$ for equalities, so the multiplier is recovered for free.",
1905: r"Ordinary penalty needs the fine to grow forever ($c\to\infty$). The augmented Lagrangian lets the tower also adjust a standing reservation $\lambda$ so that the plane lands exactly on the boundary with only a moderate fine.",
1906: r"Instead of ever-tighter fines, the tower nudges the reservation $\lambda_{k+1}=\lambda_k+c\,h(x_k)$ by small corrections until the plane touches down on the boundary exactly: tuning, not brute force.",
}

KEY = (" <br><br><em>Key for the whole Midterm 3 deck.</em> $\\Omega$ = legal airspace; $f$ = altitude (gravity is $-\\nabla f$); "
       "walls $g_j\\le0$ = mountain walls; wall push = multiplier $\\mu_j$; $T_\\Omega$ = legal headings; "
       "air-traffic control = the projection operator.")

FIX = {cid: {"personalConnection": A + t} for cid, t in _TXT.items()}

_BASE_901 = ("<strong>The airplane analogy.</strong> The Feasible Set (Ω) is the legal airspace and runway tarmac where you are permitted to fly and taxi. "
             "The Active Set names the mountain walls your plane is physically parked against right now. The mountains on the other side of the valley "
             "(inactive constraints) have zero effect on your immediate next move. ")
FIX[901] = {"personalConnection": _BASE_901 + KEY}
